model.en:
Top function at one level:<br/><pre><code>function renewSubscription(sub, now) {
  if (sub.status !== 'active') return;
  if (daysUntilExpiry(sub, now) &gt; RENEWAL_WINDOW_DAYS) return;
  gateway.charge(sub.cardToken, sub.amountCents); // already policy-level — left inline
  extendPeriod(sub, nextInvoiceNumber(sub));
}</code></pre>One step down:<br/><pre><code>const MS_PER_DAY = 86_400_000;
function daysUntilExpiry(sub, now) {
  return Math.floor((sub.expiresAt - now) / MS_PER_DAY);
}
function nextInvoiceNumber(sub) {
  return 'INV-' + String(sub.invoiceSeq + 1).padStart(8, '0');
}
function extendPeriod(sub, invoiceNo) {
  db.update('subs', { id: sub.id }, { expiresAt: sub.expiresAt + 30 * MS_PER_DAY, invoiceNo });
}</code></pre>Behaviour is unchanged; the policy is now four skimmable lines, and the three machine details — date math, invoice string, SQL — each live one level down behind a name the policy can read. <code>gateway.charge</code> stayed inline because wrapping it would add a hop and name nothing new.

